@PACK Scout Skills
@ENTRY knots|Knots Worth Knowing
A few knots handle almost everything. The bowline makes a loop that won't slip or jam, even after heavy load: form a small loop, the rabbit comes up the hole, around the tree, back down. Use it to secure a line around anything.

Two half-hitches ties a rope to a post and holds under tension but unties easily. The taut-line hitch grips when loaded but slides to adjust, perfect for a tent line or tarp.

The square knot joins two ropes of equal size: right over left, left over right. Get those few down and you can rig a shelter, hang food, or haul a load.
@END
@ENTRY fire|Making Fire
Fire needs three things: heat, fuel, and air. Gather tinder (fluffy dry stuff that catches a spark), kindling (twigs from pencil to thumb thick), and fuel wood, before you light anything. Collect three times more than you think you need.

Build a teepee or log-cabin shape with gaps for air. Light the tinder low, on the side the wind comes from, and feed kindling slowly as the flame grows.

The real lesson isn't fire, it's preparation: do the boring gathering first, and the exciting part takes care of itself. Always drown a fire cold before you leave it.
@END
@ENTRY navigation|Finding Your Way
The sun rises in the east and sets in the west. Around noon it sits due south in the northern hemisphere, so shadows point roughly north. That alone keeps you oriented without any tools.

A compass needle points to magnetic north. To follow a bearing, turn until the needle lines up with the housing arrow, then walk toward the travel arrow. On a map, contour lines close together mean steep ground, far apart means flat.

Pick a handrail to follow, a stream, ridge, or trail, so you move in a straight line instead of wandering in circles, which lost people naturally do.
@END
@ENTRY firstaid|Handling an Emergency
The scout's rule in any emergency is to act in order: check the Scene is safe before you rush in (or there will be two victims), then check the Person, then handle the worst problem first. Calm and order beat panic and speed every time.

For hands-on steps, open the First Aid guide in GuideBooks: it walks you through bleeding, burns, choking, CPR, shock, sprains, breaks, heat, and cold as step-by-step checklists you can follow under pressure.

The scout skill is preparation: know where the first aid kit is, know who is trained, and practice before you need it. In a real emergency you fall to the level of your practice, not the height of your intentions.
@END
@ENTRY lashings|Lashings and Camp Gadgets
Rope plus a few poles builds almost anything at camp: a table, a tripod, a flagpole, a gateway. The skill is the lashing, wrapping cord to bind poles together tightly.

A square lashing joins two poles at right angles: start with a clove hitch on one pole, wrap around both poles three or four turns, then take two or three "frapping" turns between the poles to cinch it drum-tight, and finish with a clove hitch. A tripod lashing binds three poles you then splay into a stable stand for a wash basin or a pot.

The magic is tension: loose lashings wobble and fail, tight ones turn sticks into furniture. Practice on short sticks at home so your hands know the moves before you're building in the rain.
@END
@ENTRY preparedness|Being Prepared
The whole idea behind being prepared is simple: think ahead about what could go wrong, and carry what handles it. It's a habit of mind, not a checklist you memorize.

A small kit covers most trouble: water, a way to make fire, a knife, a light, a way to stay warm and dry, and a little food. The same thinking applies to a school project or a road trip, ask what you'll need before you need it.

People who plan ahead look lucky. They're not; they just did the thinking early, when it was calm, instead of late, when it was a crisis.
@END
@ENTRY weather|Reading the Sky
You can predict weather hours ahead by watching clouds. High wispy clouds mean a change is coming in a day or so. Low, dark, stacking clouds mean rain soon. A ring around the moon often means rain within a day.

Red sky at night is a sailor's delight; red sky at morning, sailors take warning. It actually works in mid-latitudes, because weather usually moves west to east.

Wind shifting and temperature dropping fast often comes before a storm. Noticing these signs is the difference between getting caught out and heading for shelter in time.
@END
@ENTRY outdoors|Leave It Better
The rule outdoors is to leave a place as wild as you found it: pack out everything you bring in, including scraps, stay on trails so you don't trample new ground, and watch animals from a distance instead of feeding them.

Human food makes wild animals sick and dependent, and a fed animal often becomes a dangerous one. Keep your food sealed and stored away from where you sleep.

The bigger principle carries everywhere: take responsibility for your impact and leave things better than you found them, whether it's a campsite, a shared kitchen, or a borrowed tool.
@END
@ENTRY knife|Using a Knife Safely
A sharp knife is safer than a dull one, because a dull blade needs force and slips. Always cut away from your body and away from the hand holding the work. Keep a clear circle of space around you when the blade is open.

Carve with controlled strokes, not big chops. When passing a knife, offer the handle first. When walking, fold it or sheath it.

Respecting a sharp tool, slowing down, paying attention, keeping it maintained, is the same respect that keeps you safe with any powerful tool later in life.
@END
@ENTRY teamwork|Working as a Team
Scouting runs on small teams where everyone has a job: someone cooks, someone gathers wood, someone sets the tent. Nothing gets done if everyone waits to be told, and nothing gets done if everyone tries to lead.

The skill is figuring out what needs doing, picking up a task without being asked, and trusting others to carry theirs. When it goes wrong, you sort it out and keep moving rather than blaming.

That habit, see the need, take a job, finish it, is what makes someone genuinely useful on any team for the rest of their life.
@END
@ENTRY confidence|Doing Hard Things
A lot of what scouting teaches is just this: you can do more than you think. The first time you sleep outside, start a fire in the rain, or finish a long hike, it feels impossible right up until it's done.

The trick is breaking a big thing into the next small step. You don't hike ten miles; you walk to the next bend, then the next. You don't build a shelter; you set one pole, then another.

Confidence isn't a feeling you wait for. It's the memory of having done hard things before, which you only build by doing them.
@END
@ENTRY bushcraft|Bushcraft: Tools and Shelter
Bushcraft is the skill of being at home outdoors with very little. It starts with staying dry and keeping your tools sharp.

SHELTER: your clothing is your first shelter - stay dry above all, because wet steals heat fast. A simple lean-to against a fallen log, roofed with a tarp or a thick layer of conifer boughs, blocks wind and rain. Build a thick bed of dry duff, grass, or boughs to sleep ON - the cold ground pulls more heat out of you than cold air does.

CORDAGE: you can make usable cord from plants. The inner bark of willow, the stalks of dogbane and nettle (dried, then twisted), all work. Split a bundle in two, twist each half away from you, then wrap them around each other the other way - a reverse wrap that locks itself tight.

BATONING AND CARVING: to split small wood, set a fixed-blade knife on the end grain and tap it through with a stick - never pound a folding knife. Carve away from your body, and never over your lap where a slip meets the big artery in your thigh. A folding saw does more, safer work than a knife for cutting wood.

The bushcrafter's mindset: slow down, think one step ahead, and let sharp tools and good technique do the work instead of muscle.
@END
@ENTRY tinder|Bushcraft: Tinder and Firecraft
A fire is only as good as the tinder that starts it. Learn to spot dry tinder even in wet weather.

BEST NATURAL TINDER: shredded juniper and sagebrush bark, dry grass, the fluff of cattail and thistle, birch bark, and pine pitch (the hard amber resin on wounded conifers) which burns even when damp. The fine dead twigs on the underside of a conifer, close to the trunk, stay dry after rain - "squaw wood."

THE FIRE LAY: tinder, then pencil-thin kindling, then thumb-thick fuel, then wrist-thick logs. Give it air from below; a fire that smothers has no oxygen, not too little fuel. Light from the upwind side so the flame leans into the fuel.

CARRY TWO WAYS to make fire always - a lighter and a ferro rod, kept dry. A ferro rod throws hot sparks even wet, but needs fine tinder to catch.

PUT IT DEAD OUT: drown it, stir the ashes, drown it again, until it's cold to the touch. In this region fire restrictions are common and wildfire is a real danger - check the rules before you strike a spark.
@END
